<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Protects React Native's bundled assets from the release resource shrinker.

  `shrinkResources true` decides what to keep by looking for references in Java
  and XML. Every image a React Native screen uses is required from JavaScript
  and resolved by name at runtime, so the shrinker sees no reference and treats
  it as dead. It does not drop the entry - it replaces the file with a 0-byte
  dummy, so the build succeeds, the APK looks normal, and the image silently
  renders as nothing. Only release builds are affected; in debug, Metro serves
  assets over HTTP and never involves the shrinker at all.

  That is exactly what happened to the UPI QR code on the collector payment
  sheet:

      @com.mohideen:drawable/assests_upi_qr : reachable=false
      Skipped unused resource .../assests_upi_qr.jpg:
        782986 bytes (replaced with small dummy file of size 0 bytes)

  Keeping every drawable rather than an explicit list is deliberate. A prefix
  list (assests_*, src_*, ...) silently stops covering any asset added under a
  new folder later, and the failure mode is a blank image in production with no
  build error. Measured cost of keeping them all here is about 60 KB beyond the
  QR itself - the shrinker's drawable savings were almost entirely that one
  file. Correctness is worth 60 KB.
-->
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    tools:keep="@drawable/*,@raw/*" />
